# Mechanical Power Path

The drivetrain transfers rotation from a single geared motor to the four driven wheels.

```text
12 V geared DC motor
        ↓
Gearbox output shaft
        ↓
1:1 external gear transmission
        ↓
Mechanical drivetrain
        ↓
Differential / axle mechanisms
        ↓
Four driven wheels
```

The encoder is integrated with the drive motor and reports shaft movement to the controller.

```text
Motor rotation
     ↓
Encoder pulses
     ↓
Software count
     ↓
Estimated shaft movement
     ↓
Motion-control decision
```

---

# Why Four-Wheel Drive?

Starlight drives all four wheels.

The purpose is not to claim that four driven wheels somehow create four times the available grip. Physics, as usual, refuses to be impressed by marketing.

Instead, 4WD allows propulsion to be distributed across multiple tire contact patches.

Potential advantages include:

- reduced dependence on one driven axle;
- better use of available traction;
- more stable propulsion during turns;
- improved ability to overcome drivetrain resistance;
- more consistent movement during forward and reverse manoeuvres.

The exact torque split between individual wheels was not directly instrumented, so no unsupported percentage is claimed.

---

## Traction Principle

The total longitudinal force is distributed among the tire contact patches:

$$
F_x = \sum_{i=1}^{4} F_{x,i}
$$

Each tire still has a limited friction capability.

A simplified tire constraint is:

$$
\sqrt{F_{x,i}^2 + F_{y,i}^2} \leq \mu_i N_i
$$

where:

- $F_{x,i}$ = driving force at tire $i$;
- $F_{y,i}$ = lateral turning force;
- $\mu_i$ = available friction coefficient;
- $N_i$ = normal force on that tire.

The same tire therefore has to share available grip between **driving and turning**.

