# Current Motor

The current drivetrain uses a:

```text
12 V
600 RPM
geared DC motor
with quadrature encoder
```

The nominal **600 RPM** value refers to gearbox-output shaft speed under the motor's stated rating conditions.

It is **not** a measured competition-track speed.

Real vehicle motion is affected by:

- motor load;
- battery voltage;
- drivetrain friction;
- tire deformation;
- wheel slip;
- turning resistance;
- surface condition.

---

# Why the Motor Was Changed

An earlier drivetrain configuration experienced a motor stall under heavy loading.

The failure chain demonstrated that a mechanical problem could propagate into the electrical system:

```text
High drivetrain load
        ↓
Motor stall
        ↓
Large electrical stress
        ↓
Motor-driver damage
        ↓
Motor / drivetrain redesign
```

The response was not simply to replace the damaged component.

The team reconsidered:

- motor speed;
- available torque;
- external gearing;
- drivetrain load;
- testing procedure;
- acceptable speed margin.

The retained configuration uses the **600 RPM geared encoder motor** with **1:1 external gearing**.

---

# External Gear Ratio

The current external drivetrain ratio is:

$$
G = 1
$$

or:

```text
1 : 1
```

This means that ideally:

```text
1 gearbox-output revolution
            ↓
1 transmission revolution
```

The gears transfer motion without intentionally increasing the nominal rotational speed.

---

## Earlier Configuration

An earlier drivetrain used a speed-increasing ratio:

$$
G = \frac{36}{24} = 1.5
$$

Conceptually:

```text
36T driving gear
       ↓
24T driven gear
       ↓
1.5× ideal speed increase
```

That configuration increased ideal output speed but reduced available torque at the wheel transmission.

The final design moved away from this approach.

---

# Why 1:1 Was Selected

Changing from the earlier:

```text
1.5 : 1 speed-increasing ratio
```

to:

```text
1 : 1
```

trades some theoretical maximum speed for greater available torque.

For the same motor torque and assumed drivetrain efficiency:

$$
\tau_{wheel} \propto \frac{1}{G}
$$

Moving from:

$$
G = 1.5
$$

to:

$$
G = 1
$$

provides approximately:

```text
50% more ideal wheel torque
```

while reducing ideal wheel speed by:

```text
33.3%
```

relative to the 1.5 ratio.

The design therefore prioritizes:

```text
Torque
Reliability
Control margin
Repeatability
```

over:

```text
Maximum theoretical speed
```

---

# Ideal Wheel Speed

With:

```text
Motor speed = 600 RPM
Gear ratio  = 1:1
Wheel diameter = 46 mm
```

the ideal wheel rotational speed is:

$$
n_w = G n_m
$$

Therefore:

$$
n_w = 1 \times 600
$$

$$
n_w = 600\text{ RPM}
$$

---

## Ideal Linear Speed

Wheel circumference is:

$$
C = \pi D
$$

Using:

$$
D = 0.046\text{ m}
$$

the ideal no-slip linear speed is:

$$
v = \frac{\pi D n_w}{60}
$$

Therefore:

$$
v =
\frac{\pi(0.046)(600)}{60}
$$

$$
v \approx 1.445\text{ m/s}
$$

So:

```text
Ideal no-slip speed ≈ 1.445 m/s
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This is a **calculated ideal conversion**, not a measured competition speed.
>
> Actual speed is lower or different depending on load, losses, wheel slip and control behaviour.

---

# Current vs Earlier Gearing

| Configuration | Wheel RPM | Ideal Linear Speed |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| **Current 1:1, 600 RPM motor** | 600 RPM | **1.445 m/s** |
| Earlier practical comparison | 800 RPM | 1.927 m/s |
| Earlier 1.5 ratio with 600 RPM motor | 900 RPM | 2.168 m/s |

The earlier system produced a higher ideal speed, but the current system gives greater mechanical margin.

