# Quadrature Encoder

The current motor includes a quadrature encoder.

The encoder reports:

```text
Rotation
+
Direction
```

through pulse transitions.

The drive software can use the count to control movement based on shaft rotation instead of depending only on elapsed time.

---

## Why Encoder Feedback Was Added

A command such as:

```text
Drive for 1.0 second
```

does not guarantee the same travel distance every run.

Movement can vary with:

- battery voltage;
- motor load;
- floor grip;
- drivetrain friction;
- initial velocity;
- tire slip.

Encoder-assisted movement instead follows:

```text
Set target count
       ↓
Reset / read encoder
       ↓
Drive motor
       ↓
Monitor count
       ↓
Target reached?
   ↙          ↘
 No           Yes
 ↓             ↓
Continue       Stop
```

---

# What the Encoder Does Not Measure

The encoder measures:

```text
motor / shaft rotation
```

It does not directly measure:

```text
robot position on the field
```

For example:

```text
Wheel rotates
      ↓
Encoder count increases
```

even if some motion is lost through:

- tire slip;
- mechanical compliance;
- drivetrain backlash.

Encoder control therefore improves repeatability but does not convert the robot into a perfect odometry system.

---

# Drivetrain Control

The drive subsystem accepts commands from the higher-level navigation software.

Conceptually:

```text
Vision / state controller
        ↓
Desired steering + movement
        ↓
Drive module
        ↓
Motor PWM + direction
        ↓
Servo command
        ↓
Physical drivetrain
        ↓
Encoder feedback
```

The low-level drive layer is also responsible for preventing software commands from exceeding useful mechanical steering limits.

---

# Steering Limits

The steering servo is constrained to a usable mechanical range.

The purpose of software clamping is to prevent:

```text
Large controller correction
        ↓
Excessive servo travel
        ↓
Steering linkage stress
        ↓
Disconnection / binding
```

Instead:

```text
Requested steering value
        ↓
Check allowed limits
        ↓
Clamp if necessary
        ↓
Send safe command
```

Mechanical limits therefore form part of the control architecture rather than being left entirely to higher-level navigation code.

---

# Forward and Reverse Operation

The drivetrain supports:

```text
Forward propulsion
Reverse propulsion
Left steering
Right steering
```

This is particularly important during parking.

Because Starlight uses Ackermann steering, it cannot rotate around its centre like a skid-steer robot.

Parking therefore uses controlled:

```text
Forward arcs
+
Reverse arcs
```

rather than an in-place rotation.

---

# Why Ackermann Was Retained

Ackermann steering adds mechanical complexity compared with skid steering.

However, it provides a vehicle-like motion model suitable for the Future Engineers challenge.

### Advantages

- reduced tire scrub compared with forcing identical front-wheel angles;
- predictable forward and reverse arcs;
- realistic vehicle steering;
- compatible with controlled parking manoeuvres;
- separates steering from propulsion.

### Costs

- additional steering linkage;
- mechanical alignment requirements;
- servo calibration;
- finite turning radius;
- cannot rotate in place;
- more complex parking geometry.

The design retained Ackermann because its advantages matched the intended vehicle architecture.

