# Engineering Trade-Off

The drivetrain decision can be summarized as:

```text
Earlier priority
     ↓
Higher theoretical speed

Current priority
     ↓
Torque
Reliability
Control
Feedback
Repeatability
```

The current drivetrain deliberately gives up some theoretical maximum speed.

That is not considered a loss if the resulting vehicle:

- accelerates reliably;
- turns predictably;
- avoids motor stalls;
- performs parking manoeuvres;
- completes the course consistently.

---

# Failure-Driven Redesign

One of the most important drivetrain lessons came from the earlier stall.

```text
Mechanical overload
        ↓
Motor stall
        ↓
Electrical stress
        ↓
Driver damage
```

This demonstrated that drivetrain design cannot be isolated from electronics.

A mechanical decision affects:

```text
Motor current
      ↓
Motor-driver stress
      ↓
Power system
      ↓
Computer stability
      ↓
Navigation reliability
```

The drivetrain was therefore treated as part of the complete vehicle system.

