# Component Testing

Before a complete autonomous run, drivetrain checks should include:

## Mechanical

- [ ] Wheels rotate freely
- [ ] Gear mesh is correct
- [ ] Shafts remain aligned
- [ ] Differential moves freely
- [ ] No gear disengagement
- [ ] Steering linkage does not bind
- [ ] Full steering range has physical clearance

## Motor

- [ ] Forward direction is correct
- [ ] Reverse direction is correct
- [ ] Motor runs without abnormal noise
- [ ] Motor does not stall under normal movement
- [ ] PWM response is predictable

## Steering

- [ ] Center command produces straight wheels
- [ ] Left command produces left steering
- [ ] Right command produces right steering
- [ ] Mechanical steering limits are respected
- [ ] Linkage remains connected throughout travel

## Encoder

- [ ] Encoder pulses are detected
- [ ] Direction sign is correct
- [ ] Counts increase consistently
- [ ] Movement target stops correctly
- [ ] Encoder-controlled motion is physically repeatable

---

# Debugging Order

If the robot moves incorrectly:

```text
1. Check wheel / gear freedom
2. Check motor direction
3. Check gear engagement
4. Check differential
5. Check steering centre
6. Check steering limits
7. Check encoder direction
8. Run low-speed encoder movement
9. Integrate with navigation controller
10. Run complete challenge
```

Changing vision gains because a gear is binding would be an admirably creative but unproductive debugging strategy.

